// leetcode 643

interview- In this question, I have to find the maximum average of a contiguous subarray of length K.

A brute-force approach would be to calculate the average of every possible subarray of size K, but that would take more time.

So, I will use the Sliding Window technique to optimise the solution.

First, I will calculate the sum of the first K elements and find their average. I will store this average in a variable called `maxiAvg`, considering it as the maximum average initially.

Then, I will initialise two pointers. The first pointer `i` will start from 0, and the second pointer `j` will start from K, which represents the next element entering the window.

Now, I will run a while loop until `j` reaches the end of the array.

For every new window, I will subtract the element that is leaving from the left side and add the new element entering from the right side.

Then, I will calculate the average of the current window and compare it with the previous maximum average using the `max()` function. If the current average is greater, I will update `maxiAvg`.

After that, I will move both pointers one position forward and repeat the same process.

Finally, I will return `maxiAvg`, which will contain the maximum average among all possible subarrays of length K.

class Solution {
public:
    double findMaxAverage(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n=nums.size();
        int sum=0;
        for(int i=0;i<k;i++){
            sum+=nums[i];
        }
        double maxiAvg=(double)sum/k;               // here sum becomes double so double/int = double 
        int i=0;
        int j=k;
        while(j<n){                               // it will give runtime error if you forget i++,j++
            sum=sum-nums[i]+nums[j];
            double avg=(double)sum/k;
            maxiAvg=max(maxiAvg,avg);
            i++;
            j++;
        }
        return maxiAvg;
    }
};
